#
# Disarm the host's hardware watchdog at shutdown: watchdogd disarms the timer only when asked to
# stop, and one left armed through a halt would reset the machine after somebody asked it to power
# off - scripts/watchdog/watchdog.sh.
#
# After the slow stop hooks, not before them. On an XGS appliance the reboot that brings a new kernel
# rebuilds the coprocessor's driver for it - os-xgs-npu's 20-octep and os-kernelfollow's
# 21-kernelfollow, up to ten minutes each - and a host that stops in that rebuild is reset only if
# the timer is still armed. Until 2026-11-06 this was 04-watchdog, before anything else, so it never
# was; os-xgs-npu's own 23-xgs-watchdog moved the same way that day. Still before 80-freebsd, which
# stops the services.
SCRIPT=/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/watchdog/watchdog.sh
[ -f "${SCRIPT}" ] || exit 0
/bin/sh "${SCRIPT}" stop > /dev/null 2>&1
exit 0
